Title: 

Why Did Abolishing Fees Not Increase Public School Enrollment in Kenya?- Working Paper 271
Abstract: 
A large empirical literature has shown that user fees significantly deter public service utilization in 
developing countries. While most of these results reflect partial equilibrium analysis, we find that 
the nationwide abolition of public school fees in Kenya in 2003 led to no increase in net public 
enrollment rates, but rather a dramatic shift toward private schooling. Results suggest this divergence 
between partial- and general-equilibrium effects is partially explained by social interactions: the entry 
of poorer pupils into free education contributed to the exit of their more affluent peers.
